After PITR (Point-In-Time Recovery) is completed, these are the main validation checks to confirm recovery was successful:
PITR Success Validation Steps
1. Confirm PostgreSQL Started Successfully
Check database status:
Bash
systemctl status postgresql
or
Bash
pg_isready
Ensure DB is accepting connections.
2. Verify Recovery Reached Target Time
Check PostgreSQL logs for messages like:
Plain text
recovery stopping before commit
completed recovery
database system is ready to accept connections
and confirm the recovery target timestamp was reached.
3. Validate Recovery Mode Ended
Run:
SQL
SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();
Expected result after successful PITR:
Plain text
f
Meaning recovery completed successfully.
4. Verify Required Data Exists
Check:
Critical tables
Recent transactions
Expected records before corruption/deletion
Example:
SQL
SELECT count(*) FROM important_table;
Validate business-critical data.
5. Verify Corrupted/Deleted Data State
Confirm:
Accidentally deleted data is restored OR
Corrupted/unwanted transaction is excluded
This is one of the most important PITR validations.
6. Run Integrity Validation
If using checksums/amcheck:
SQL
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS amcheck;

SELECT bt_index_check(indexrelid)
FROM pg_index
WHERE indisvalid;
This validates index consistency.
7. Review PostgreSQL Logs
Check for:
PANIC
corruption
invalid page
WAL replay failure
checksum errors
Example:
Bash
grep -Ei "PANIC|ERROR|corrupt|checksum" postgresql.log
8. Validate Application Connectivity
Ensure:
Application connects successfully
Read/write operations work
No functional errors observed
Simple Management Statement
“PITR recovery validation was successfully completed by verifying database startup, WAL replay completion, recovery target consistency, data integrity, and application connectivity.”
Or shorter:
“PITR success was validated through recovery completion checks, data validation, and integrity verification.”